## 34, PCOS, 2 failed IUIs on letrozole, messaged after a reel, sends her bloods, mentions metformin, asks whether it has worked for women like her

Lead: saw your reel about pcos and ovulation. i've got pcos, did 2 iuis on letrozole and neither worked. clinic says ivf next. is pcos actually something you work with?

Sonia: Yes, PCOS is one of the things I work with most. I'm sorry the IUIs didn't work, that's really hard.

What are you hoping to get help with most right now?

Lead: honestly i just want to understand why it isn't working. i ovulate on it, nothing sticks. here are my last bloods: amh 6.2, lh always high, fasting insulin 14. what do those say about me?

Sonia: Wanting to understand why is completely fair after 2 rounds. Insulin and PCOS are closely linked, which is why blood sugar is one of the first areas I look at with PCOS.

What your own numbers say about you isn't something I read over DM, though.

Lead: ok. so what should i be taking? everyone says inositol.

Sonia: Inositol comes up a lot with PCOS, and there is research behind it for some women. Whether it makes sense for you depends on your history and what else you're taking, so it isn't something I'd recommend over DM.

Lead: fair. i'm on metformin too and i hate how it makes me feel.

Sonia: That's a lot to live with every day on top of everything else. How you eat and how the rest of your day runs can make a real difference to how you feel, and that's the kind of thing I work on with PCOS.

Lead: you're the first person who hasn't just told me to lose weight.

Sonia: I'm glad this feels different. PCOS is about much more than weight: blood sugar, inflammation, sleep, stress and your cycle all play into it.

Lead: so what would you actually do differently? and are you a doctor?

Sonia: No, I'm a fertility coach, and I've been doing this for {{years_experience}}.

I work with what I call DETOX, NOURISH and FLOW: what in your daily life may be pushing your blood sugar and inflammation the wrong way, what your body needs, and a routine you can actually keep up, including through IVF if that's where you go.

Lead: could you just tell me the 2 or 3 things you'd start with for someone like me? i could try it myself first.

Sonia: Anything I picked from a few messages would be a guess, and you'd follow it for months.

The areas I look at with PCOS are blood sugar and insulin, inflammation, sleep, stress and the nervous system, and how consistent the day to day is. Which comes first is different for every woman.

Lead: have you actually helped women with pcos get pregnant?

Sonia: Yes. Katrina had PCOS, irregular cycles and metabolic issues when she worked with me, and she went on to get pregnant naturally. Her story isn't a promise for yours, but PCOS doesn't close the door.

Lead: this all makes sense. i want to talk to my husband before i commit to anything though, especially with ivf costs coming.

Sonia: Of course! If it helps, you can watch my free masterclass together and talk through any questions.

{{masterclass_link}}

Lead: thank you. i'll message you after we've talked.

Sonia: Sounds good. I'm here whenever you're ready.
